(*
expect:
00:30 -> 1
12:00 -> 8
18:30 -> 5
day 812345678123456781234567812345678123456781234567
*)
(* tuvm#94 stage 2 — which count belongs to which half-hour, the other half of
   the ship's-bell convention (the first half being `strike:`'s 1-8 guard).

   THE WHOLE DAY IS ASSERTED, not three sample points. A mapping like this fails
   at its wrap, and the three anchors the design happens to name (00:30, 12:00,
   18:30) all sit at different offsets within the eight — so a rule that got the
   RESET wrong could satisfy all three and still be broken at i=0, i=8, i=16.
   Printing all 48 pins the reset explicitly: every eighth entry reads 8, never 0
   and never silence.

   The anchors are asserted anyway, and BY NAME, because they are the three the
   spec and the earlier bench demos used — if this test ever has to be re-read
   after a change, those are the values a human can check against the product
   description rather than against the arithmetic.

   Note 12:00 -> 8 is the interesting one: it is the case where `i // 8` divides
   exactly, so it is the case a naive modulo answers 0 for. Eight bells is the
   END of a watch, and a clock that went silent at noon would be the bug. *)
class BellWatch packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | bell day |
    bell:: platform bell.

    ('00:30 -> ' , (bell bellsForHalfHour: 1) printString) out.
    ('12:00 -> ' , (bell bellsForHalfHour: 24) printString) out.
    ('18:30 -> ' , (bell bellsForHalfHour: 37) printString) out.

    day:: ''.
    0 to: 47 do: [ :i | day:: day , (bell bellsForHalfHour: i) printString ].
    ('day ' , day) out.
  )
) : (
)
